# Copy to .env at the repo root and edit. .env is gitignored; this file is the documentation.
#
# Every name carries EXPO_PUBLIC_. Metro inlines a variable into the mobile bundle only when it
# does, and desktop has no such constraint - so desktop reads the same spelling rather than each
# variable existing twice and drifting. An explicit environment variable always beats this file.

# Which network. mainnet | testnet | devnet. Unset means mainnet.
# Each network gets its own database directory, so switching never mixes accounts and going back
# does not lose the one you had.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_NETID=mainnet

# --- devnet ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The only devnet value that has to be configured: the seed node's oxend RPC. Every seed node the
# app hands libsession - pubkey, IPv4 address, both storage ports - is read from it at startup,
# because a devnet regenerates those keys whenever it is rebuilt and anything written down here
# would go stale without saying so.
#
# A bare host, host:port or http://host:port; the port defaults to 1280. Take it from the "IP:RPC"
# column of any service node row. Required when the netid is devnet: with it missing the app
# attaches nothing at all rather than falling back to mainnet.
#
# Note oxend binds this RPC to the devnet host's own address while the storage servers bind
# everything, so a machine that can reach the swarm can still fail to reach this.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_DEVNET_SEED_URL=192.168.1.114:1280

# --- the devnet's file server, published on :8000 ----------------------------
# Both of these or neither. Without them a devnet uploads attachments to the *mainnet* file
# server: only testnet has one of its own, so production storage is what libsession falls back to,
# and the upload succeeds with nothing afterwards to show it happened.
#
# The key is the server's **Ed25519** pubkey: libsession derives the X25519 one it encrypts onion
# requests with. `docker compose logs fileserver` prints the Ed25519 key first and the X25519 one
# beside it: this is the first. Both are 64 hex characters; the bridge refuses an X25519 key at
# start when it can, and when it cannot, uploads fail at the server.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_FILE_SERVER_URL=http://192.168.1.114:8000
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_FILE_SERVER_ED25519_PUBKEY=<64 hex>

# --- which transport ---------------------------------------------------------
# onion-requests | session-router. Unset takes the network's default: session-router on mainnet
# and testnet, onion-requests on devnet - which is the only thing a devnet can use, because
# Session Router refuses one outright.
#
# The two share only a ten-method interface: uploads, downloads, connection status and retry
# behaviour are separate implementations on each side. Set this to exercise the transport you ship
# on a network that has it, rather than inferring it from a devnet run.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_ROUTER=onion-requests

# --- development -------------------------------------------------------------
# The fake libsession: no network, no C++, a seeded history. `pnpm dev:fake` sets it for you.
# It draws thirty dev photographs and will not start without them: `pnpm fake-media:fetch` puts
# them in ~/.cache/session-app/dev-media, and `pnpm --filter @session/mobile fake-media:push`
# copies them onto an Android device or a booted iOS simulator.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_FAKE=1
#
# Which world it starts in: friends (the default), showcase (every attachment shape), backlog (a
# long history of attachments still to download), newcomer (no account yet), crowd, or rows (one
# conversation per state a conversation row draws).
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_FAKE_FIXTURE=backlog
#
# How big crowd is: peers, and messages per peer. Setting the peers alone picks crowd.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_FAKE_CROWD_PEERS=1000
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_FAKE_CROWD_HISTORY=1
#
# How much slower a transfer runs, so the bar and the ring stay on screen long enough to watch.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_FAKE_SLOWDOWN=10
#
# A start that fails, to reach the startup-failure screen on purpose, and how it fails: 1 (not a
# database), corrupt, locked, full, key-unavailable or key-missing. The first two and key-missing
# draw the unreadable explanation, the rest the restart one. See FAKE_FAILED_STARTS.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_FAKE_FAILED_START=1
#
# Keeps a database key on the fake as the real bridge does, so key custody and the clear past its
# failures can be reached by hand: on desktop, put a file named session.db in the network's data
# directory and remove db-key.bin beside it to see key-missing.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_FAKE_KEY_CUSTODY=1

# How much of libsession's own logging to forward, per category:
#
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_LOG=<level>[,<category>=<level>]...
#
# A bare level is the default for every category; the pairs override it, in any order. Worth
# turning up when something fails for no visible reason - a send that ends in `failed` carries no
# reason, and the reason is in these lines. It is also the only place the network and the transport
# actually chosen are reported.
#
# A flat `debug` is not useful: quic and session-router produce thousands of lines a second and
# bury whatever you were looking for. Turn up the one you need instead:
#
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_LOG=warn,network=debug          # which network, which transport, paths
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_LOG=warn,client=debug           # what the bridge asked libsession to do
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_LOG=warn,snode_pool=debug       # swarm and cache refreshes
#
# The ones worth knowing are `network`, `client`, `snode_pool`, `configs` and `core`; the router
# and the transports declare another sixty-odd. A name nothing declares is passed through and
# does nothing, because libsession makes a logger for it that matches no line.

# Which build variant the app reports, over what the build says about itself: how to see what
# another variant shows, such as the Huawei description of fast mode. One of the names in
# BUILD_VARIANTS in packages/app/src/buildVariant.ts; anything else is ignored. Never in a release.
# docs/BUILD_VARIANTS.md has the list.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_BUILD_VARIANT=huawei

# Feature flags. Every flag in the shared table has one of these, derived from its name, and any of
# 1/true/yes/on turns it on. Anything else falls through to the default, so a typo cannot enable
# something by accident.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_FLAG_RECOVERY_PHRASE_SHORT=1
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_FLAG_RECOVERY_PHRASE_LONG=1
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_FLAG_RECOVERY_PHRASE_LANGUAGE=1
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_FLAG_FORCE_ONBOARDING_TABLET_DESIGN=1
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_FLAG_FORCE_FAST_MODE_AVAILABLE=1
# EXPO_PUBLIC_SESSION_FLAG_DISABLE_ANIMATIONS=1
